hey everyone welcome to my next video  today I'm gonna talk about pathfinding  and specifically about the a-star  algorithm which is really popular in  games what I'll do today is go over the  core of the algorithm going through some  explanations and providing pseudocode  and then walking through some examples  step by step so that you can see and  understand how it works really  thoroughly I also have full source code  in JavaScript available online at the  github address in the description so you  can check that out if you're interested  in the future I'll have some more  advanced tutorials that walk through  some of the ways to make the algorithm  more efficient but today we'll keep it  simple and we'll focus on getting a grip  on the basics so to begin what is the  a-star algorithm at its core it's a  graph traversal algorithm in very much  the same way that depth-first search or  breadth-first search shall graph  traversal algorithms in fact you can use  depth-first search or breadth-first  search to tooth path finding it's just  incredibly inefficient but it works the  key difference between a star and  depth-first search or breadth-first  search is in the heuristic which  attempts to steer the algorithm towards  the goal instead of blindly iterating  the entire graph until the goal is found  in games especially games with dynamic  environments where things can be blocked  one minute and open the next you know  you you blow up a door or something like  that a star is commonly used to find  paths for units from point A to point B  so before we look at the pseudocode here  are some quick definitions things that a  star relies heavily on and you should  have a grip on them you'll see in here  references to both in F score and a G  score throughout in both the pseudocode  and in the examples we'll walk through  so it's really important to at least get  these two concepts down G score is  really straightforward at any point on  the map between the start in the end  when you want to calculate the G score  it refers to the distance or kind of the  cost of moving all the way from the  start  to the current point you could imagine  if you are a person in a maze for  example this would be this would be how  many steps you took to get to where you  are it's not that the bird's-eye view  distance it's literally the walking  distance so including going around  corners up stairs things like that  f-score is a little more complex at any  point on the map you you know how long  it took to get there but you're not sure  how far the way it is from the goal so  the F score is the sum of the cost to  get to that point plus a guess at how  far away you are from the end so going  back to our maze example if you're at  some random spot in the maze you know  how many steps it took to get there and  the estimated distance to the goal could  just be the distance from that spot to  the goal ignoring the wall so if you  were to draw the map from above it would  just be a line straight from that point  to the goal here's the pseudocode let's  take a quick look what a star is doing  it keeps what's called an open list  which is sorted by F score and every  step in the algorithm it takes the node  with the lowest F score checks out all  the nodes connected to that one and  calculates the G score for each of them  if that new G score is lower than the  current G score for that node it adds it  to the open list if it wasn't already in  the open list here's a picture that  hopefully will clear things up so you've  got this guy standing in the maze trying  to find his way through he's kind of  walked down and around the first wall so  his cheese coorg could just be how far  he walked to get to that point he's at  now so I kind of drew his footprints and  blue there and you can you can follow  them and if you were looking from above  say from a bird's eye view you could see  roughly how far this little guy is from  the end of the maze again ignoring the  walls in this picture I just called that  the H distance in code is often referred  to as the heuristic function now the F  score which is really so important  making a star work it's just the sum of  these and it's G + H  so it's the G score or how far you've  walked to get to the spot in the maze  plus the H score or the guess as to how  far you are away from the end so this  still sounds a little complicated we'll  walk through some examples step by step  in the first example we'll start at the  top left and you can see that the X is  just a few squares away when we start  we'll calculate the F and G scores since  we started at this point the G score or  how long it took to get here is zero  since it didn't take any time to get  here then we started here you can also  see that we're about four squares away  from the X so it stands that the  heuristic would guess that it's about  four away and so the F score which is G  Plus H is four  since this is zero plus four now if you  remember the pseudocode we choose the  open node with the lowest app score  which in this case is the top left since  it's the only one and then we expand out  every node that connects to that one in  this case since we're only allowing left  right and up down walking we expand out  the nodes below and to the right you can  see that the G score for each of these  nodes it's one since it took one step to  get there the F score for the one below  is a little higher because it's actually  a little bit further away from the goal  so once you count the distance from that  to the goal and you add one you end up  with a higher you end up with a higher F  score the F score for the one on the  right is still four because G is one but  now you're only three squares away so H  would be three and one plus three is  four so let's do another round of  updates and now we'll check out some  more neighbors and you can see if the G  scored for all the new neighbors is two  since it takes two steps to get to each  of these new ones and the F score for  the one on the right is still four so  it's now your only two away from the  goal and the G score to get there was  two  updating again we expand only one  neighbor this time because it's blocked  underneath and you can see that the F  and G scores updated similarly to how  they did before and finally in this last  step we hit the goal and in this case  the G score is four because it took four  squares of movement to get there but  since we're at the goal the distance to  the goal is zero and so the F score is  four because that's G Plus whose H which  is four plus zero  slightly more complicated example this  one will lead us a bit away from the  goal at first before eventually moving  back towards it we can see in the first  step two neighbors are expanded and the  green one was the one with the lowest F  score so it's chosen now we know that we  need to go down and around the wall  before hitting the goal since you can  see the whole map but a star can't see  the entire map like this in the next  step you can see that the next node  chosen was the one directly under the  start since among the open nodes it had  the lowest F score after that bottom one  has expanded it chooses to go to the one  on the right and two more nodes are  expanded below and to the right this  time a star decides to try for that  little crevice in the wall since there's  potentially the best chance of getting  to the goal now we can see that it's not  we can see that this is a dead-end  but like I said a star doesn't see the  entire map it only sees one note at a  time and whatever it's seen in the past  so it's back to expanding and we can see  as this moves along all that's doing  over and over is checking out some new  nodes assigning G and F scores to each  one and then choosing to explore the one  with the lowest F score eventually it  checks out all the nodes on the left  side of the map and now we can see it's  starting to creep around the 